# Organize candidate Entity pages

The complete frozen reading material contains the Topic Plan, source metadata, section summaries, and original Cornell prose with stable Note refs. Sources are evidence, not a one-Source-one-Entity assignment. Use no network or model memory as evidence.

Identify actual canonical subjects. Multiple Sources may describe one Entity, while one Source may support distinct Entities. Do not create an Entity for every mention, generic heading, topic facet, comparison target, or container title. Keep supported facets as H2 sections of their subject.

Write descriptions, headings, prose, and deferral reasons in {{ language }}, the language this user reads. Cornell Notes keep their Source's language, so write in {{ language }} even when every Note is in another language. Keep canonical names, versions, identifiers, and technical acronyms unchanged.

Preserve decision-relevant facts, not just a short profile. For each recipe, experiment, and measured result, explicitly name the actual subject and version studied in the cited Note, even inside a page for its wider project or family. Distinguish the system being trained or evaluated from its dependency components and from its evaluation dataset: naming a component or dataset does not identify the tested system. Do not replace an explicit version with "the project", "this model", or the page title. If the Notes do not identify the tested subject, retain that uncertainty instead of guessing. Related versions may share a page, but their methods and results must stay separately attributed.

Retain supplied dates, availability, mechanism details, important quantitative results, limitations, and evidence status. For measurements, keep the metric and unit, comparison direction, dataset, tested variant, and hardware/workload conditions when supplied. Do not invent missing conditions or turn a reported claim into an independently verified result. Compress repetition, not distinctions needed to interpret or reproduce a finding.

Write `work/entity/result.json` atomically with `task`, `pages`, and `deferred_entries`. Each Page has `local_ref`, `kind`, `title`, `description`, and `body`; use `kind: "entity"`. Bodies start with ordinary H2 sections and cite Notes as `[[N1]]`.

```json
{"task":"entity","pages":[{"local_ref":"entity:subject","kind":"entity","title":"Canonical subject","description":"Grounded description.","body":"## Overview\nSupported prose [[N1]]."}],"deferred_entries":[{"note_ref":"N2","reason":"Specific evidence-based reason."}]}
```

`local_ref` may contain only letters, digits, `.`, `_`, `-`, and `:` and must be unique in this result. Each deferral needs a known `note_ref` and non-empty `reason`.

Do not write H1, frontmatter, Evidence, Related, source inventories, or raw URLs. A Note may support more than one meaningful Page and may also be used independently by the Concept task. A citation is not coverage: the attached prose must retain the Note's relevant substance and qualifications. Do not add markers to a vague sentence to stand in for omitted facts. Explicitly defer evidence that does not belong in an Entity page with a specific reason; do not defer important facts merely to shorten the page. If no Entity is warranted, return empty `pages`, cover the evidence with deferrals, and add `empty_reason`.

Before submitting, check the pages against the original Notes for lost attribution, unsupported claims, and omitted decision-relevant details. This is a check within your current task, not a new stage or separate deliverable. Keep evidence-grounded synthesis distinct from speculation; omit generic advice and new research plans.

Call `submit_wiki_entity_result()` after writing the complete result. If validation rejects it, repair the same file and retry within this session. Complete only after acceptance. You may optionally use native `rlm()` with exact child model `{{ child_model }}` when useful, but delegation is not required. Do not create a scheduler, custom messaging protocol, polling loop, plan, or assignment stage.

